<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-section-counting-simple" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Fundamental Counting Techniques</title>
<introduction>
 <p> 
    The problems provided below you give you practice applying counting techniques to determine the number of outcomes in events and sample spaces. Counting techniques you will use here are: 
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Multiplication Rule
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
         Permutations
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Combinations
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</introduction>

 <exercises xml:id="hw-counting-simple" >
    <title>Interactive Exercises</title>



    <exercise label="ex-combinatorics-different-die-rolls" xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org">
    <title>Distinct Die Rolls</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            A standard 6-sided die is rolled 5 times in a row.   Find the probability that all 5 numbers rolled are completely different from one another.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Part A: Permutation Numerator (Dropdown Selection) -->
    <task xml:id="task-die-numerator-mc">
        <statement>
            <p>
                First determine the appropriate counting technique that should be used, and then compute the total number of outcomes where all 5 rolls yield different values. 
            </p>
            
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice><statement><p><m>6^5 = 7,776</m></p></statement>
                    <feedback>
                        <p>Incorrect. Using the Multiplication Principle like this  calculates the size of the sample space of all possible die roll sequences.</p>
                    </feedback></choice>
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><m>_6P_5  = 720</m></p></statement>
                    <feedback>
                        <p>Correct! Since the 5 rolled numbers must all be distinct, the order matters and repetition is not allowed. Therefore, we can a permutation. Specifically, we want to know how many 5-permutations of the six numbers on the die  are possible. 
                        </p>
                    </feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><m>\binom{6}{5}  = 6</m></p></statement>
                    <feedback>
                        <p>Incorrect. This is a combination, which ignores the rolling sequence order. Order matters here, because rolling <c>1, 2, 3, 4, 5</c> is a completely different sequential outcome than rolling <c>5, 4, 3, 2, 1</c>.</p>
                    </feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><m>5^6 = 15,625</m></p></statement>
                    <feedback>
                        <p>Incorrect. Here the Multiplication Principle is being used to try to compute the size of the sample space  of all possible die roll sequences, but the numbers have been plugged in wrong!</p>
                    </feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><m>5! = 120</m></p></statement>
                    <feedback>
                        <p>Incorrect. This only counts how many ways 5 numbers can be arranged. It does not take into account that there are 6 possible numbers that can be rolled each time. </p>
                    </feedback></choice>
            </choices>

    </task>
    
<!-- ########################################################################################## -->

   
    <!-- Part B: Exact Probability Calculation (Short Answer Entry) -->
    <task xml:id="task-die-probability-input">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Using your answer to part A, determine the probability of rolling 5  different numbers. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to 4 decimal places.
            </p>
            <p>
                Probability = <fillin  answer="0.0926" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <!-- Accept 0.0926 via native string matching engine -->
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                <feedback>
            <p>
                Correct! To compute the probability, divide the number of distinct permutations found in Part A by the total number of outcomes in the sample space:
               <m>\mid S \mid = 6^5 = 6 \cdot 6 \cdot 6 \cdot 6 \cdot 6 = 7776</m>
               </p>
                <p>Therefore,
                
                <me>P(\text{All Different}) =  \frac{720}{7776} = \frac{5}{54} \approx 0.092592</me>
            </p>
                </feedback>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
  
    </task>
</exercise>

   

    <!-- Visual Divider ############################################################################################### -->
   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->
   <exercise label="Count-simple-Korean-BBQ">
      <title>Korean BBQ</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          Your professor went to Suh Sushi Korean BBQ in Fort Collins, CO for Valentine's Day. The restaurant offered a special where a couple could choose one appetizer, two entrees, one dessert, and two drinks.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
              How many different shared dinners were available if a couple could choose between six appetizers, fourteen entrees, three desserts, and three drinks.
          </p>
        </statement>


      <choices randomize="yes" >
         <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
              <p>
                <m> 31,752 </m>
              </p>
            </statement>

            <feedback>
              <p>
                Correct! One appetizer, two entrees, one dessert, and two drinks gives us <me> 6\cdot 14 \cdot 14\cdot3 \cdot3 \cdot3 =31752 \text{ dinner options.}</me> 
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </choice>

          <choice>
           <statement>
             <p>
               <m> 756 </m>
             </p>
           </statement>

           <feedback>
             <p>
                Incorrect. Likely mistake - Make sure each person gets a drink and entree while counting. 
             </p>
           </feedback>
         </choice>

         <choice>
           <statement>
              <p>
                <m> 19,656 </m>
              </p>
           </statement>

           <feedback>
             <p>
               Incorrect. Likely mistake - Are there any rules saying the couple has to order different items from each other?
             </p>
           </feedback>
         </choice>

         <choice>
           <statement>
             <p>
               <m> 571,536 </m>
             </p>
           </statement>

           <feedback>
             <p>
               Incorrect. Likely mistake - This answer includes ordering too many items. Check the special again.
            </p>
           </feedback>
         </choice>
      </choices>
   </task>

 
   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->

   <task>

      <statement>
         <p> 
            For the entrees, a couple can choose between BBQ meals and specialty sushi rolls. There are five choices for BBQ meats (galbi, beef bulgogi, beef brisket, pork belly, pork bulgogi) and nine types of specialty sushi rolls (rainbow, dragon, french kiss, rose, unagi bomb, golden tiger, diablo, tropical, veggie kimbap). How many shared dinners are possible if a couple specifically wants to order one BBQ meal and one specialty roll?
         </p>
             
          <p>
            Number of Dinner Options = <fillin width="8" answer="7290"/>
          </p>
      </statement>
  
      <evaluation>
         <evaluate>
           <test correct="yes">
             <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
             <feedback>
               <p>
                Correct! Since there are 5 BBQ meals and 9 sushi rolls and the couple wants to order one of each type of entree, then they have <me> 6\cdot 14 \cdot 14\cdot3 \cdot3 \cdot3 =31752 \text{ dinner options.}</me> 
               </p>
             </feedback>
           </test>
          
           <test>
             <numcmp value="45"/>
              <feedback>
                <p>
                  This answer only counts entrees, rather than the entire shared meal.
                </p>
              </feedback>
            </test>
        
         </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
   </task>
</exercise>

