   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->





 
 <exercise label="count-simple-5x6-array" >
    <title>5-by-6 Array</title>
    
      <statement>
        <p>
          Thirty items are arranged in a 5-by-6 array as shown below. Calculate the number of ways to form a set of three distinct items such that no two of the selected items come from the same row or column.
       </p>

       <image source="Images/5x6-array.png" width="60%">
       <description>Grid with 5 rows and 6 columns. Each grid cell contains an item, labeled A1 through A30.</description> 
      </image>


      
      </statement>
<hint>
  <p>
    Does order matter here?
  </p>
</hint>
      <choices randomize="yes" >
         <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
              <p>
                <m> 1,200 </m>
              </p>
            </statement>

            <feedback>
              <p>
                Correct! For the first item, we have 30 options to select from. For the second item we have only 20 options, since we have to remove all items that were in the same row and column as the first item. Similarly, there are only 12 options for item 3. However, the calculation <m> 30\cdot 20\cdot 12</m> takes into account the order of the items. So, we need to divide by <m>3!</m> to get the number of unique sets of three items.
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </choice>

          <choice>
           <statement>
             <p>
               <m> 1,575 </m>
             </p>
           </statement>

           <feedback>
             <p>
                Incorrect. Likely mistake - Did you remove enough options after the first and second item selection? No two items can come from the same row or column.
             </p>
           </feedback>
         </choice>

         <choice>
           <statement>
              <p>
                <m> 4,060 </m>
              </p>
           </statement>

           <feedback>
             <p>
               Incorrect. Likely mistake - No two of the selected items can come from the same row or  column.
             </p>
           </feedback>
         </choice>

         <choice>
           <statement>
             <p>
               <m> 2,000 </m>
             </p>
           </statement>

           <feedback>
             <p>
               Incorrect. Likely mistake - Make sure all items come from different rows and  columns.
            </p>
           </feedback>
         </choice>

         <choice>
           <statement>
             <p>
               <m> 7,200 </m>
             </p>
           </statement>

           <feedback>
             <p>
               Incorrect. Likely mistake - This answer treats a selection such as <c>A1 A15 A29</c> as different from <c>A29 A15 A1</c>, when they are actually the same set of chosen items.
            </p>
           </feedback>
         </choice>
      </choices>
   </exercise>

