<exercises xml:id="hw-counting-simple-turn-in" >
   <title>Written Exercises</title>


<exercise label="counting-simple-10digit-phone-num">
<title>Unique Phone Numbers</title>

<introduction>
  <p>
    Consider ten-digit phone numbers in the United States.
  </p>

</introduction>

  <task>
  <statement>
    <p>
      How many different ten-digit phone numbers are possible? 
      </p>
      <p>
Note: Creating a mathematical model that accurately reflects the real world is important but often  difficult. Model assumptions should always be stated. In this case, we assume that the first digit cannot be zero or one, and the fourth digit cannot be zero. 

    </p>
  </statement>
  </task>
  
   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->
  <task>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Toll free numbers are telephone numbers that can be called at no charge to the person placing the call. For example, calling a toll free number would allow a caller to reach a businesses out of the area without being charged a long-distance fee. Toll free numbers start with a distinct three-digit code, such as 800. </p>
      <p>How many ten-digit phone numbers are possible if we exclude toll free numbers starting with 800?
    </p>
  </statement>
  </task>
</exercise>


    <!-- Visual Divider ############################################################################################### -->
   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->

<exercise label="counting-simple-bday-problem">
<title>Birthday Problem</title>

<introduction>
  <p>
    	Consider an experiment that consists of recording the birth month for each of 7 randomly selected persons. 
  </p>

</introduction>
    
   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->
  <task>
  <statement>
    <p>
      	Find the number of outcomes in the sample space, <m>S</m> for this experiment. 
      </p>

  </statement>
  </task>

  <task>
  <statement>
    <p>
      	If we assume that months are equally likely, what is the probability that each person had a different birth month? 
    </p>
  </statement>
  </task>
</exercise>


   </exercises>

</section>